# Rule: Release Stigmer OSS

## Purpose

When triggered, determine the correct semver bump, create annotated tags with detailed release notes, and push them to origin — triggering all downstream CI release pipelines.

## Rule Type

**Action Rule** - Executes git tag and push operations

## When to Use

- When user asks to "release", "cut a release", "tag a release", or "make release"
- After a set of changes on `main` are ready to ship

## Execution Steps

### 1. Identify the Previous Release

```bash
git tag -l "v*" | sort -V | tail -n1
```

Report the latest tag to the user (e.g. "Previous release: **v0.1.2**").

### 2. Collect Commits Since Last Release

```bash
git log --oneline <previous-tag>..HEAD
```

List commits for the user so they can see what's included.

### 3. Determine the Version Bump

Apply **Conventional Commits** rules to choose `patch`, `minor`, or `major`:

| Condition | Bump |
|-----------|------|
| Any commit message contains `BREAKING CHANGE:` or has a `!` after the type/scope | **major** |
| Any commit has type `feat` | **minor** |
| Only `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `ci`, `refactor`, `perf`, `style`, `test`, `revert` | **patch** |

Present the reasoning and proposed new version (e.g. "`feat` commit found → minor bump → **v0.2.0**"). Ask the user to confirm before proceeding.

### 4. Check for Uncommitted Changes

```bash
git status --short
```

If there are uncommitted changes, warn the user and ask whether to commit first or abort.

### 5. Compose Release Notes

Build a Markdown-formatted annotated tag message. Group commits by Conventional Commit type:

```
Release v<NEW_VERSION>

## What's Changed

### Features
- **<scope>**: <description>

### Bug Fixes
- **<scope>**: <description>

### CI/CD
- **<scope>**: <description>

### Documentation
- **<scope>**: <description>

### Chores
- <description>

**Full Changelog**: https://github.com/stigmer/stigmer/compare/<PREV>...<NEW>
```

Rules for the notes:
- Omit sections that have no commits (e.g. skip "### Features" if there are no `feat` commits)
- Skip merge commits and project-management docs commits (changelogs, session checkpoints, next-task updates) from the notes — they add noise
- Use imperative mood and keep each bullet to one line
- Include scope in bold when available

### 6. Create Tags

Create three annotated tags (mirroring the `make release` target):

```bash
git tag -a "sdk/go/v<NEW>"     -m "Release sdk/go v<NEW>"
git tag -a "mcp-server/v<NEW>" -m "Release mcp-server v<NEW>"
git tag -a "v<NEW>"            -m "<full release notes from step 5>"
```

The `sdk/go` and `mcp-server` tags get short messages; the main `v*` tag gets the full release notes.

### 7. Push Tags

```bash
for t in "sdk/go/v<NEW>" "mcp-server/v<NEW>" "v<NEW>"; do
  echo "  pushing $t"
  git push origin "$t"
done
```

### 8. Confirm

After pushing, print the CI summary (same as `make release`):

```
Tags pushed. CI will handle:
  - Protos to BSR                  (release.buf.yaml)
  - CLI binaries + GitHub release  (release.cli.yaml)
  - Desktop app installers (draft) (release.desktop.yaml)
  - @stigmer/* npm packages        (release.npm-libs.yaml)
  - Go SDK (go get)                (sdk/go tag auto-cached by proxy.golang.org)
  - stigmer + stigmer-protos PyPI  (release.python-sdk.yaml)
  - MCP server binaries + Docker   (release.mcp-server.yaml)
```

## Important Notes

- **Never force-push tags** — if a tag already exists, stop and tell the user
- **Always confirm** the version bump with the user before creating tags
- **Clean working tree required** — do not tag with uncommitted changes unless the user explicitly approves
- If the user specifies a bump type (`patch`, `minor`, `major`), use it; otherwise infer from commits
